#!/usr/bin/env ssc
---
name: named-arg-defaults
description: a named argument for a defaulted parameter binds BY NAME, and the remaining parameters come from their defaults
backends: [int, js, jvm]
---

# Named arguments bind by name, not by position

`BUGS.md` `standard-tier-named-arg-skip-default`. Both self-hosted fronts dropped the label and
lowered the value positionally, and they did it *differently*: F emitted a short application
(`ssc: arity: 4 expected, 2 given`, loud) while the legacy front put the value in the **first
omitted slot** (`b=C1 c=C0 d=D0`, silent, exit 0). The filed report also claimed that naming the
FIRST defaulted parameter worked — re-measured 2026-07-28, it did not.

Every line below is a shape the fix has to get right, and each one failed differently before it:
name a middle parameter, name the last, name the first, name two, name two **out of order**, and
the two shapes that always worked (no named args at all, and a fully positional call) as the guard
that the ordinary paths were not disturbed.

Object-method named args (`O.m(1, c = 9)`) are deliberately **absent**: the v1 interpreter — which
is this suite's `int` golden — binds them to the wrong parameter too, so pinning the correct answer
here would make the case red for the golden's own defect rather than for the compilers'. That
divergence is filed separately as `v1-interp-object-method-named-arg-wrong-slot`, and the shape is
covered against the self-hosted oracle in `specs/v2.2-p6.5-fsub.sh` (`narg_objm`).

```scalascript
def f(a: String, b: String = "B0", c: String = "C0", d: String = "D0"): String =
  "b=" + b + " c=" + c + " d=" + d

case class P(x: Int, y: Int = 2, z: Int = 3)

println(f("x", c = "C1"))
println(f("x", d = "D1"))
println(f("x", b = "B1"))
println(f("x", c = "C1", d = "D1"))
println(f("x", d = "D1", b = "B1"))
println(f("x"))
println(f("x", "B2", "C2", "D2"))
println(P(1, z = 9).y)
println(P(1, z = 9).z)
println(P(1).z)
```
